The '''Geedi Period''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Xilligii Geedi'', from ''geedi'', "march" or "journey") was the founding era of the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], spanning approximately 1,000 to 500 BCE. It encompasses the development of iron smelting, the unification of the highland clans, and the kingdom's expansion from its [[Lake Haro]] heartland to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. The period is named after the '''Geedi Dynasty''', the founding royal house, though the number and identities of individual rulers are poorly attested: the only writing system in use was the [[Farta Geedi]] logographic script, which was not suited to detailed historical records.

== Sources ==

The Geedi Period predates the adoption of alphabetic writing on the peninsula. Knowledge of the era derives from three sources: [[Farta Geedi]] logographic inscriptions at the [[Buur Waaq]] shrine and [[Badalga-Jir]], which provide fragmentary royal dedications and administrative records; [[Dalmar Period|Dalmar-era]] stone inscriptions in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]], written one to five centuries after the events they describe; and archaeological evidence, including the remains of ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' forts, [[Bir (currency)|bir]] currency hoards, and pottery sequences. The Dalmar retrospective accounts are formulaic and may reflect dynastic propaganda.

== Origins ==

Dalmar-era inscriptions identify the founder of the Geedi Dynasty as '''[[Cagmadhige]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "the first step"), a semi-legendary figure said to have unified the highland clans through control of newly developed iron-smelting technology. No independent corroboration of Cagmadhige's existence has been established, and scholars are divided on whether the name refers to a historical individual, a composite of several early chieftains, or a mythological figure.

Cagmadhige is credited with establishing the three pillars of the Ufyen state: the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrison system, the worship of [[Waaq]] with the king as chief priest, and the capital at [[Badalga-Jir]]. He is traditionally said to have been crowned at the summit of [[Buur Waaq]]. The dynasty name ''Geedi'' ("march" or "journey") may refer to his founding campaign or to a migration tradition.

== Expansion ==

The Geedi kings consolidated control over the iron mines of the [[Kushish]] highlands and the mountain passes connecting the southern coast to the [[Northern Basin]]. From the core territory around [[Lake Haro]], they expanded outward over several centuries, establishing the capital at [[Badalga-Jir]] on a defensible peninsula within the lake and the commercial gateway at [[Jowhar]].

At the period's height (~700 BCE), the kingdom controlled territory from the highlands to the coast, exacting tribute from river-valley communities along the [[Buran River|Buran]] and [[Galbeed River|Galbeed]]. The [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys further west remained independent. The '''[[Dagaalka Buran]]''' ("War of the Buran", ~700–650 BCE) extended Ufyen control to the mouth of the [[Buran River]], bringing the sites of the future cities of [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] and [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] under tributary rule.

The Geedi administration constructed a network of fortified hilltop garrisons (''[[Kaas|kaas]]''), stone-paved roads, and irrigation works around Lake Haro. The [[Bir (currency)|bir]], a crescent-shaped iron currency, was standardised during this period.

== Collapse ==
{{main|Ancient Southwestern Kaftia#Collapse and interregnum}}

The kingdom's territorial extent strained its capacity for governance. The diverse subject populations — [[Hiddid]] highland communities, lowland farmers, coastal fishers, and semi-nomadic [[Bariites]] on the eastern fringes — resisted tribute extraction through a series of rebellions beginning in the 6th century BCE. Simultaneously, [[Dakari people|Dakari]] war bands from the [[Northern Basin]] raided through the mountain passes. The last attested Geedi ruler, '''[[Kulmis]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "he who gathered"), is named in Dalmar-era inscriptions that describe the final collapse. Under Kulmis, the dynasty lost control of the highland provinces and the Buran corridor. The kingdom fragmented, and the Ufyen state contracted to its Lake Haro heartland, governed by a succession of short-lived warlords until the rise of the [[Dalmar Period]] around 400 BCE.

== Legacy ==

The Geedi Period established the political, religious, and economic structures that defined the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] through its subsequent periods. The ''kaas'' fort network, the [[Waaq]] state cult, the [[Farta Geedi]] script, and the [[Bir (currency)|bir]] currency all originated in this era. Later dynasties — particularly the [[Warfaa Period|Warfaa]] — invoked the memory of the Geedi as a golden age.

